\section{Aesthetics}{

\code{geom_point_3d()} supports all the same aesthetics as \code{\link[ggplot2:geom_point]{ggplot2::geom_point()}}, plus \code{z}:
\itemize{
\item \strong{x}: X coordinate (required)
\item \strong{y}: Y coordinate (required)
\item \strong{z}: Z coordinate (required)
\item \code{alpha}: Transparency
\item \code{colour}: Point border color
\item \code{fill}: Point fill color (for certain shapes)
\item \code{shape}: Point shape
\item \code{size}: Point size (gets depth-scaled when \code{scale_depth = TRUE})
\item \code{stroke}: Border width for shapes with borders
}
}

\section{StatPoint3D returns the following computed variables}{

\itemize{
\item \code{x_raw}, \code{y_raw}, \code{z_raw}: Original values before discrete-to-numeric conversion
}
}

\section{Depth Scaling}{

The depth scaling uses an inverse relationship with distance, following the
mathematical relationship: \code{apparent_size = base_size * reference_distance / actual_distance}

This creates realistic perspective where:
\itemize{
\item Objects twice as far appear half as big
\item Objects twice as close appear twice as big
\item The center of the plot volume renders at exactly the user-specified size
}
}

\section{Point Rendering}{

ggcube uses shape-aware rendering for improved stroke behavior:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Simple shapes (0-20, characters):} Use \code{size} for fontsize, \code{stroke} for border width, no fill
\item \strong{Complex shapes (21-25):} Use ggplot2's approach: \code{size + 0.5*stroke} for fontsize to prevent gaps
\item Both size and stroke are depth-scaled when \code{scale_depth = TRUE}
\item All shapes preserve stroke depth scaling and parameter control
}
}

\section{Reference elements}{

To show where points lie in 3D, combine a point layer with layers that
project the points onto faces of the panel:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=geom_dropline_3d]{geom_dropline_3d()}} draws lines from the points to faces.
\item \code{geom_point_3d(position = position_on_face())} draws the points on
faces, as upright markers, or with \code{flat = TRUE}, as markers lying in
the faces.
}

All of these layers are depth-sorted together.
}

